    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that \"intrinsic value\" is just a subjective way to dress up a lack of objective meaning. you say the middle chapters have value, but that's just human bias/sentimentality. if you look at it purely from a logical or mathematical standpoint, if the end state is always zero, then the path taken to get there doesn't change the sum. if the total sum is zero no matter what, then the choice to end the sequence early is mathematically identical to letting it run its course. it's a neutral decision. how can you say one is better than the other when the net impact is exactly the same?\n Balance the equation for me. if the end is always nothing",
      "source": "evaluator",
